 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Compression Ratio:** Achieves strong compression ratios of about 1% to 3% of the original data set size [S1]. For example, on the CELLS dataset (4.0GB), CSV with $b=64$ and rANS achieves 2.805% [S1].
-- **Throughput:** High throughput up to gigabytes per second for both compression and decompression (1.5 to 3 GB/s observed) [S1].
-- **Memory Overhead:** Temporary memory overhead for pyramid generation is approximately 14% of the brick size [S1].
-- **Scalability:** Supports out-of-core processing for datasets that do not fit into RAM, as compression is brick-wise [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Lossless, supports adaptive LOD, GPU-friendly decompression, high parallelization potential [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Neighbor references cannot cross brick borders, which may reduce compression efficiency for large homogeneous regions spanning multiple bricks [S1]. Naive parallelization (T threads for T bricks) can lead to suboptimal CPU utilization for larger brick sizes due to divergent processing times [S1].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+*   **Compression:** Throughput is 1.5 to 3 GB/s [S1]. Parallelization uses T threads for T bricks, which is naive and leads to suboptimal utilization for larger bricks due to divergent processing times [S1].
+*   **Memory:** Temporary memory overhead for pyramid generation is ~14% of the brick size [S1]. Decompression is in-place, requiring only the output array for the target LoD [S1].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+*   **Compression Ratio (CR):** Achieves 1% to 3% of original size [S1].
+    *   CELLS (4.0GB): 2.805% (b=64, rANS) [S1].
+    *   FIBER (5.7GB): 0.899% (b=64, rANS) [S1].
+    *   CORTEX (927.7GB subvolume): 1.590% (b=64, rANS) [S1].
+*   **Comparison:** Outperforms HDF5 (gzip), PNG, and Neuroglancer. Comparable to Compresso+LZMA for CELLS/CORTEX, but significantly better for FIBER [S1]. 8-30x faster than Compresso+LZMA [S1].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** High throughput, lossless, supports adaptive LoD, GPU-friendly decompression [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Neighbor references do not cross brick borders. Naive parallelization can lead to load imbalance [S1].
